{
  "meta": {
    "purpose": "Maps free-text food-log item names (from bfl_meals.vision_items_json and .description) onto canonical plant species, so the chef-weekly skill can count DISTINCT plants eaten in a trailing 7-day window against the American Gut 30-plants/week finding (McDonald 2018, mSystems).",
    "why_a_count": "A plant count is immune to the portion-estimation problem that makes photo-derived micronutrient math indefensible. You either ate a leek or you did not - grams are irrelevant. This file is the only genuinely new data work v1 needs.",
    "canonicalization_rules": [
      "Same botanical species eaten in the same culinary form collapses to one key: 'scallion' and 'spring onion' and 'green onion' -> one plant; 'red bell pepper' and 'bell pepper' -> one plant.",
      "Refined-grain forms of the same grain collapse: all wheat products (bread, pasta, couscous, tortilla, bagel, panini, flatbread) count as ONE plant (wheat). Rice forms count as ONE plant (rice).",
      "Err toward NOT double-counting near-identical strings. A count that overstates variety is worse than one that understates it - understating is honest, overstating is theatre.",
      "Distinct varieties that are eaten distinctly and that the American Gut protocol would treat as different plant types stay separate (e.g. jalapeno vs bell pepper, green beans vs black beans, kale vs cabbage).",
      "Composite dishes ('pizza with mushrooms and ham', 'bolognese sauce') are NOT resolved here - the free-text mapper only catches ingredients it can see as tokens. The prompt (Claude) decomposes composites and adds the plants it can identify. This file handles the deterministic single-ingredient case.",
      "Composite catch-alls that name no species ('mixed vegetables', 'grilled vegetables', 'salad', 'garnish') are listed under non_plants, NOT as a plant. They resolve to None and score zero. Counting them as one plant inflates the score with a token that identifies nothing - the same reason 'mixed leafy greens' was collapsed into lettuce."
    ],
    "definition_of_plant": "Per the American Gut protocol, a 'plant' includes vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, nuts, seeds, herbs, and spices. Herbs and spices count - that is why 30 is achievable rather than punishing.",
    "not_medical": "This map supports an observational variety count, not any nutritional-status claim. See the skill prompt's medical framing.",
    "source": "American Gut Project: McDonald D et al. 2018. 'American Gut: an Open Platform for Citizen Science Microbiome Research.' mSystems 3(3):e00031-18. Finding: participants eating >30 distinct plant types/week had greater microbial diversity than those eating <=10. Association, not causation.",
    "last_reviewed": "2026-07-29",
    "honest_count_note": "The count must not be allowed to inflate. Three corrections in the 2026-07-29 pass each removed inflation: 'green onion' collapsed into 'onion' (both Allium cepa); 'mixed leafy greens' collapsed into 'lettuce'; 'mixed vegetables (unspecified)' demoted to non_plants. The trigger was a run reporting 35/30 and 29/30 on consecutive weeks against a target the log showed being reached once in four months. An inflated counter is a participation trophy, not a target. Extend the plants map from unresolved_tokens, and re-read this rule before adding a synonym that would split one species into two."
  },
